  <h2>The 2012 Heritage and the Path to Southeast Asia</h2>
  <p>
    SleepTech was founded in Osaka in 2012, in a city that has always occupied a distinctive position in Japan's cultural geography — more pragmatic than Tokyo, more commercial than Kyoto, with deep roots in both craft manufacturing and medical technology. The founding team's vision was informed by a specific observation about where Japan's caregiving crisis was heading and what it would require.
  </p>
  <p>
    The founders recognized that the same demographic forces reshaping Japan in 2012 would reshape the rest of Asia within a generation. South Korea, Taiwan, China, Thailand, and Vietnam all faced rapidly aging populations and similar pressures on traditional family-based care structures. The technology designed to serve Japanese families navigating the gap between oyakōkō values and urban migration realities would, with appropriate cultural adaptation, serve families across Asia facing analogous transitions.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why SleepTech has invested as heavily in cultural adaptation as in technical development. The Thai version of the YumePad app was designed in consultation with Thai families and draws on the concept of <em>กตัญญู</em> (gratitude toward parents and ancestors) as its organizing emotional frame. The Vietnamese version draws on <em>đạo hiếu</em> — the Confucian-Vietnamese tradition of filial piety that shares deep structural roots with oyakōkō but has its own particular expressions in Vietnamese family life. The Japanese product remains the design origin point, but it is not exported wholesale: each cultural context receives a product designed to feel native to the values it serves.
  </p>

  <h2>The Ethical Architecture of Care Technology</h2>
  <p>
    Any technology that monitors an elderly person — regardless of how gently, how quietly, how well-designed — must grapple with the ethics of surveillance in vulnerable populations. Japan's cultural context, where respect for elders is foundational, makes this tension particularly acute. Monitoring an elderly parent without their full understanding and consent is not oyakōkō. It is, at best, paternalism; at worst, a form of violation.
  </p>
  <p>
    SleepTech's data governance model is built on a principle the team calls "data hospitality" — borrowed loosely from the Japanese concept of omotenashi, the hospitality that serves without being asked and without drawing attention to the service. Data hospitality means that the person being cared for owns their data absolutely: they decide what is shared, with whom, and for how long. Family members are guests in the data, not co-owners. If the elderly person wants to withdraw data access from their children at any time, they can. If they want to review what their children see, they can see exactly that view.
  </p>
  <p>
    This architecture reflects a recognition that the relationship between adult children and elderly parents in the context of care technology is not — and should not be — a relationship of supervision. It is a continuation of the relationship of mutual respect and obligation that kaigo demands. The technology serves that relationship. It does not replace it.
  </p>

  <h2>What Families Are Saying</h2>
  <p>
    In the decade-plus that SleepTech has been operating, the feedback from Japanese families who have adopted the YumePad system consistently returns to a theme that the design team finds both validating and humbling: the technology gave them permission to sleep.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not permission to stop caring — permission to stop worrying without stopping caring. The distinction, as one user put it, is the difference between a long-distance relationship and an abandoned one: "I still care just as much. I just don't have to prove it every night by lying awake. The sensor does the watching, so I can sleep — and wake up better able to be a good daughter."
  </p>
  <p>
    This is, in the end, what kaigo in the twenty-first century may need to look like: not a wholesale replacement of human presence with technological surveillance, but a thoughtfully designed partnership in which technology handles the continuous, reliable, passive monitoring that no human can sustainably perform — and frees human attention for the irreplaceable things that only humans can give.
  </p>

